## Notes on scope

Part A is open source under the Open Source Definition. **Part B is not** — it is
source-available. Describe them accordingly: "MIT-licensed toolchain,
source-available IDE" is accurate; "the whole project is open source" is not.

This licence restricts copying the IDE's **code and assets**. It does not, and
cannot, stop someone from building a different editor that looks like it or works
like it, written from scratch. Copyright protects expression, not design
decisions.
